(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_semiconductorelectronics=[{id:"neet-2026-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"p-n junction biasing, depletion width",q:"Three identical p-n junction diodes D\u2081, D\u2082, and D\u2083 are connected across a battery as shown in the figure. If the width of the depletion regions of D\u2081, D\u2082 and D\u2083 are W\u2081, W\u2082 and W\u2083, respectively, then the correct option is:",o:["W\u2083 = W\u2081 > W\u2082","W\u2083 > W\u2082 > W\u2081","W\u2082 > W\u2081 = W\u2083","W\u2081 > W\u2082 > W\u2083"],c:1,s:`D\u2081 is forward biased.
Depletion region of a forward-biased diode (W\u2081) is the minimum.
D\u2083 is reverse biased.
Depletion region of a reverse-biased diode (W\u2083) is the maximum.
D\u2082 is unbiased.
Its depletion region (W\u2082) lies between W\u2081 and W\u2083.
So W\u2083 > W\u2082 > W\u2081.`},{id:"neet-2026-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Zener diode in reverse bias",q:"An ideal Zener diode with breakdown voltage of \u22123 V is reverse biased with a negative input voltage V\u1D62 = \u22125 V. The magnitude of voltage difference between point B and A is:",o:["2 V","1 V","0 V","3 V"],c:0,s:`The input voltage magnitude (5 V) is greater than the Zener voltage magnitude (3 V), so the Zener diode is in breakdown.
The voltage across the Zener is V_zener = 3 V.
Total voltage V_total = 5 V.
The rest of the voltage falls across the resistor between B and A.
|V_BA| = 5 \u2212 3 = 2 V.`}]})();
